1. Check Docker version

docker --version


2. Check existing Docker images

docker images


3. Create and run a new container

docker run -d -p 5001:5000 --name test-python-container my-python-app


4. Check the running container

docker ps


5. Test the application in browser

http://localhost:5001


6. Test the application using PowerShell

Invoke-WebRequest http://localhost:5001


7. Display only the returned content

(Invoke-WebRequest http://localhost:5001).Content


8. View container logs

docker logs test-python-container


9. Inspect the container

docker inspect test-python-container


10. Enter the running container

docker exec -it test-python-container /bin/sh


11. List files inside the container

ls


12. Check the current directory

pwd


13. Exit the container

exit


14. Check the container after exiting

docker ps


15. Stop the container

docker stop test-python-container


16. Check all containers

docker ps -a


17. Start the stopped container

docker start test-python-container


18. Check the running container again

docker ps


19. Stop the container before removing

docker stop test-python-container


20. Remove the container

docker rm test-python-container


21. Verify container removal

docker ps -a


22. Check that the Docker image still exists

docker images
